 instance's test and demos as task state. A new-instance notice resets the state even if its task ID recurs; never reuse demos, deductions, or guessed rules from an earlier instance in the same conversation. On every task turn, emit exactly one permitted action JSON object as the entire reply: `submit` with a valid grid, `request_demos`, or `finish` only after the instance is over. No commentary, tools, or fences.\n2. Parse exact dimensions and compare input/output geometry before proposing a rule. For expanded outputs, segment by the factor ratios (e.g. output height/input height and width/input width), then compare candidate tiles or blocks against the input, including rotation/reflection and axis reversal. Check the proposed mapping cell-by-cell against every demo; do not infer simple scaling or mirroring from output size or resemblance alone. For sparse recoloring tasks, record the complete changed-cell mask (both recolored and unchanged candidates) and test a concrete rule against every marked and unmarked cell; visual alignment or apparent gaps alone is not validation.\n3. For a stamp/copy rule, extract the marker's non-background coordinates relative to its bounding box. If demos show rotated or reflected copies, enumerate the unique dihedral orientations (four rotations and their reflections) before searching. Match all target placements against the original input, collect and deduplicate their cells, then apply changes; do not let earlier edits create new matches. Check that this procedure reproduces every demo's changed-cell mask before using it on the test.\n4. Before requesting a demo, state internally the specific unresolved rule choice it would distinguish, and compare the expected information against its one-point cost. First re-derive candidate rules from all existing examples, including unchanged objects and regions—not merely visible edits. Request only when a plausible pair of alternatives predicts different outcomes on the next example; otherwise do not spend a demo on an open-ended hope for clarity. After incorrect feedback, never repeat the same prediction unchanged. If feedback says the grid is invalid, first audit JSON syntax, dimensions, row widths, and cell values; if it is a valid-but-wrong output, treat it as evidence that at least one rule assumption is false. Compare predicted edits cell-by-cell with the test and recheck the full changed-cell mask against every demo. Identify the falsified assumption before choosing a correction. Request a genuinely discriminating demo only if plausible alternatives remain unresolved; never submit a guessed variation.\n5. Apply only validated edits to a copy of the test. Verify dimensions, every row width, values, and changed coordinates against the demos. The final line must contain exactly one action JSON object; finish only after the instance is over.\n"}],"action":{"type":"submit","grid":[[9,8,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,2,9,9,9,9,9],[9,8,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,2,9,9,9,9,9],[9,8,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,2,9,9,9,9,9],[9,8,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,2,9,9,9,9,9],[9,8,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,2,9,9,9,9,9],[9,8,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,2,9,9,9,9,9],[9,8,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,2,9,9,9,9,9],[9,8,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,2,9,9,9,9,9],[9,8,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,2,9,9,9,9,9],[9,8,9,9,9,7,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,2,9,9,9,9,9],[9,8,9,9,9,7,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,2,9,9,9,9,9],[9,8,9,9,9,7,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,2,9,9,9,9,9],[9,8,9,9,7,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,2,9,9,9,9,9],[9,8,9,7,7,9,7,9,9,9,9,9,9,2,9,9,9,9,9],[9,8,9,7,7,7,7,9,9,9,9,9,9,2,9,9,9,9,9],[9,8,9,9,7,7,7,9,9,9,9,9,9,2,9,9,9,9,9],[9,8,9,7,7,7,7,9,9,9,9,9,9,2,9,9,9,9,9],[9,8,9,7,7,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,2,9,9,9,9,9],[9,8,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,2,9,9,9,9,9],[9,8,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,2,9,9,9,9,9]],"correct":false}},{"step":194,"kind":"observation","phase":"act","t0":1792598677.611,"t1":1792598690.841,"alias":"task-15506a5c","message":"Feedback since your last action:\nYour submission was INCORRECT. Wrong attempts so far: 1.\n\nSame instance. Task id: task-15506a5c\nTest input (20x19)